    "iosManual.introPrimary": "A complete guide to getting started, configuring, and using Checking on iPhone.",
    "iosManual.introSecondary": "The images use fictitious simulator data. Numbered highlights show exactly where to tap; permissions and times may vary according to the device Settings.",

    "iosManual.sections.introduction.title": "Introduction",
    "iosManual.sections.introduction.lead": "Checking is an attendance system designed for workplaces registered by administrators.",
    "iosManual.sections.introduction.item1": "During an evaluation, the app obtains the device position and sends the coordinates to the API only to compare that position with registered workplace areas and decide the correct activity. Outside those areas, no workplace is identified.",
    "iosManual.sections.introduction.item2": "Checking does not create or display a history of the user's route. Coordinates do not appear in the activity history; if the internet is unavailable, a pending evaluation may remain protected on the device only until it is synchronized.",
    "iosManual.sections.introduction.item3": "The system includes the Android (Kotlin) and iOS (Swift) apps, the central API, the employee Web App, the administration Website, the Transport Dashboard, and the other support, privacy, and management pages. They all share the same rules and attendance state.",
    "iosManual.sections.introduction.item4": "When a manual or automatic check-in or check-out is accepted, the API also handles the FORMS required by Petrobras. The first check-in of the day and each eligible check-out are submitted once per FORMS-enabled project; the server prevents duplicates.",
    "iosManual.sections.introduction.callout": "Important: to determine that the user is outside a registered location, the iPhone still needs to obtain its position at that moment. This does not mean tracking or storing the user's route.",

